+index aaede4c..baea8c6 100644
+--- a/DEPLOYMENT_GUIDE.md
++++ b/DEPLOYMENT_GUIDE.md
+@@ -343,6 +343,8 @@ Previously, if a title wasn't yet in the Jellyfin library, `defineStreamHandler`
+ 
+ **New module — `seerr.js`:** mirrors `jellyfin.js`'s client pattern. `requestMedia(tmdbId, mediaType)` calls `POST /api/v1/request` — for TV, requesting all seasons (`seasons: "all"`), since Stremio's per-episode granularity doesn't map cleanly onto a one-click "request this show" action.
+ 
++**Bug hit and fixed during testing — duplicate requests on repeat clicks.** Live testing showed tapping "Request via Seerr" twice on the same title (22 seconds apart) created TWO separate "Requested" entries in Seerr instead of the second one being rejected. Root cause, confirmed by reading Seerr's actual source (`server/entity/MediaRequest.ts`, same in `seerr-team/seerr`/`fallenbagel/jellyseerr`/`sct/overseerr` - the duplicate check is unchanged across the fork lineage): the "does a request already exist" check is a plain `SELECT` immediately followed by an `INSERT`, with no database transaction, no unique constraint on `(tmdbId, mediaType, is4k)`, and no locking - two POSTs close together can both pass the SELECT before the first one's row commits. This addon's original 409-based dedup assumed Seerr's own check was reliable; it isn't. **Fix, in `seerr.js`:** `requestMedia()` now guards against duplicates two ways before ever POSTing: (1) an in-memory `Map` tracking `tmdbId:mediaType` combos requested by this addon process in the last 30 seconds - instant, no network call, catches the exact "impatient repeat tap" scenario that caused the bug; (2) a live pre-check against Seerr's own `GET /api/v1/movie/{tmdbId}` (or `/tv/{tmdbId}`) endpoint, inspecting `mediaInfo.status` (skip if `AVAILABLE`/`PARTIALLY_AVAILABLE`) and `mediaInfo.requests[]` (skip if any entry has status `PENDING`/`APPROVED`) - this catches "already requested in a previous process lifetime" (e.g. after a restart cleared the in-memory guard), which the in-memory check alone can't. **A second bug was hit while building this fix**: the pre-check's first version omitted the `/api/v1` prefix on its request path and shared the same 15-second timeout as every other Seerr call - live testing showed a single `/request` call taking over 20 seconds and returning a 502, because the mis-pathed pre-check call hung for the full 15s before falling through to the real POST. Fixed by correcting the path and giving the pre-check its own dedicated 3-second timeout (`precheckClient`, separate axios instance from `client`) - since the pre-check is a nice-to-have optimization layered on top of the in-memory lock (the actual primary guard), it's fine for it to fail/skip fast rather than block the whole request. Verified live: two rapid requests for the same fresh title (`tt0110357`, The Lion King) resolved in 2.8s and 0.38s respectively (the second hitting the in-memory lock, never reaching Seerr), and Seerr's Requests page confirmed exactly one entry was created.
++
+ **Bug hit and fixed during testing — IMDb-to-TMDb resolution:** the first implementation of `findByImdbId()` searched Seerr's own `/api/v1/search?query=<imdbId>` endpoint, assuming TMDb's search backend would resolve a raw IMDb-id-shaped string directly. Live testing (`tt0068646`, The Godfather — an unambiguous, definitely-indexed title) proved this wrong: `/search` is confirmed (by reading Seerr's own source, `server/routes/search.ts` / `server/api/themoviedb/index.ts`) to be a plain title-text search against TMDb's `/search/multi`, with no external-id matching at all — it silently returned zero relevant results for an ID-shaped query. **Fix:** call TMDb's own `/find/{imdbId}?external_source=imdb_id` endpoint directly instead (the same endpoint Seerr's backend uses internally via `getMediaByImdbId()`/`getByExternalId()`, confirmed in `server/api/themoviedb/index.ts` — it's just never exposed as its own public Seerr route). This uses TMDb's own public "read" API key, which Seerr/Overseerr/Jellyseerr all ship hardcoded in their own open-source repos (`431a8708161bcd1f1fbe7536137e61ed`) — not a secret tied to this deployment, so no separate TMDb account/key registration is needed. Overridable via an optional `TMDB_API_KEY` env var if you'd rather use your own.
+ 
+ **Bug hit and fixed during testing — stale/invalid Seerr API key:** the first API key copied from Seerr's Settings → General page 403'd on `/api/v1/search` (and every other authenticated endpoint) while the unauthenticated `/api/v1/status` and `/api/v1/settings/public` endpoints worked fine — confirmed via direct `curl` (bypassing this addon entirely) and by reading Seerr's own auth middleware (`server/middleware/auth.ts`): it does a strict `req.header('X-API-Key') === settings.main.apiKey` string comparison, and a mismatch means `req.user` never gets set, causing every permission check downstream to 403 regardless of which permission is actually required. Regenerating the key in Seerr's UI and using the fresh value fixed it immediately — the original key had simply gone stale/invalid server-side.
