+Subject: [PATCH 15/16] fix: whitelist requestable ids instead of blacklisting
+ Adult ids
+
+Stremio queries every installed addon for a stream, regardless of
+which addon's catalog the user actually found the title through - so a
+content id from a completely unrelated addon's catalog (confirmed
+live, 2026-09-16: an "OnlyPorn" addon) reaches this addon's stream
+handler exactly the same as one from this addon's own Movie/Series
+catalogs. Blacklisting "known Adult ids" (adultItemIds, added
+2026-09-16) only ever protected ids this addon's own Adult catalog had
+listed - it did nothing for ids sourced from any other addon, which
+fell through to the default "offer Seerr" behavior since they simply
+weren't on the blacklist.
+
+Replaced with requestableItemIds: a whitelist populated only by this
+addon's own Movie/Series (non-Adult) catalog handler. buildRequestStream
+and the /request route's belt-and-suspenders check now only allow a
+Seerr request for an id actually in this set - Adult content, and any
+id from a different addon, are excluded by construction rather than by
+trying to enumerate every case that shouldn't be offered. Same
+disk-persistence approach as before (data/requestable-item-ids.json,
+gitignored) so it survives this addon's own periodic restarts.
+
+Verified live: an id resembling a third-party addon's (never seen via
+this addon's own catalogs) now correctly gets an empty stream list
+(no Seerr offered) and a 403 from /request, while real movie ids seen
+via the Movie catalog remain requestable as before.
